Lauren Lovelace
USUNNY ECOSOC Counselor
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. Chair.
The United States wishes to place on record our deep and growing concern regarding the direction and effectiveness of the Second Committee. This year, in an unprecedented step, we have opposed most individual resolutions as well as the Committee’s Program of Work. This reflects our conviction that the Second Committee, in its current form, is failing to deliver meaningful, actionable outcomes and is instead perpetuating a cycle of repetitive, unproductive mandates.
We have consistently called for revitalization and reform. Yet, the Committee continues to advance a “business as usual” agenda, disregarding the urgent need for streamlining, prioritization, and fiscal discipline. The proliferation of non-consensus resolutions this year is a clear signal that the Committee’s credibility and utility are at risk.
The United States opposes resolutions that recycle contentious or outdated language, reaffirm frameworks such as the Financing for Development Seville Compromise of which there is no consensus, and the United States does not recognize or support, and create new, duplicative mechanisms that undermine real results. We do not support the repeated reaffirmation of the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the SDGs as a matter of course, nor do we accept aligning international financial institutions, official development assistance, or domestic policy with frameworks we do not endorse.
We are concerned by proposals across multiple resolutions that would expand UN authority into areas best managed by sovereign states and specialized institutions, interfere with market-based solutions, and undermine investor confidence. We object to mandates that would increase bureaucracy, raise costs, and distract from the Committee’s core economic and financial mission.
The United States stands for national sovereignty, fiscal responsibility, market-based solutions, and practical economic outcomes that deliver real benefits to people. Our unprecedented opposition to all individual resolutions and the Program of Work is a clear signal: the Second Committee must change course if it is to remain relevant. We urge all Member States to join us in demanding a Second Committee that is focused, efficient, and capable of delivering results.
Thank you for your attention to this matter.
