Lawrence Foster
USUNNY ECOSOC Advisor
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Mr. Chair,
The United States wishes to state, unequivocally, that we do not support global debt-relief efforts, as referenced under this resolution, until countries can take a serious attempt at right sizing their balance of payments.
First, the resolution advocates for broad-based debt relief and restructuring, extending even to middle-income countries. This approach threatens the integrity of sovereign debt markets and could have far-reaching negative consequences for access to finance. It also places undue and inappropriate pressure on private creditors, many of whom are based in the United States, to accept losses or terms that are inconsistent with established legal, risk, and market frameworks.
Second, the resolution encourages the use of state-contingent debt instruments and the scaling up of debt swaps for development goals. These mechanisms can introduce uncertainty and risk for creditors, potentially reducing the attractiveness of sovereign debt and raising borrowing costs for all countries, including those the resolution claims to help.
Third, the resolution’s call for an intergovernmental process on debt at the United Nations is unacceptable. Debt issues are best addressed through established technical institutions, not through politicized intergovernmental forums that lack the necessary expertise and risk undermining effective solutions. Furthermore, the United States wishes to clarify that the World Bank, IMF, and Paris Club have all previously covered intergovernmental processes on debt, with existing mandates to do so. This has occurred despite sovereign debt burdens remaining largely stagnate despite previous debt relief.
The resolution also contains language that could be interpreted as seeking to regulate U.S.-based credit rating agencies and private sector actors in ways that are inconsistent with our laws, regulatory frameworks, and market principles.
Finally, the resolution inextricably links the Sustainable Development Goals, despite the SDGs failings to advance broad economic growth. We do not consider SDGs a guiding framework for our international engagement. We therefore must firmly reject the resolution before us, as this resolution inappropriately includes unacceptable language on topics unrelated to debt, and on these topics, we refer you to our long-form 2C Explanation of Position on our website.
In summary, the United States votes no. We urge Member States to reconsider this resolution and to respect the integrity of our financial institutions and markets.
Thank you.
