Alexandra Teisan
USUNNY ECOSOC Advisor
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. Chair,
The United States wishes to place on record our deep and growing concern regarding the direction and effectiveness of the Second Committee. This year, in an unprecedented step, we have opposed most individual resolutions as well as the Committee’s Program of Work. This reflects our conviction that the Second Committee, in its current form, is failing to deliver meaningful, actionable outcomes and is instead perpetuating a cycle of repetitive, unproductive mandates.
Chair, President Trump has delivered a very clear message: the United Nations and so many nations of the world have gotten wildly off track, exaggerating climate change into the world’s greatest threat. This practice will no longer be tolerated.
Rather than identifying the real drivers of food insecurity in many parts of the world, this resolution turns to climate change as a foil when the focus should be on issues like poor rule of law, poor regulatory regimes, poor resource management, poor infrastructure, and civil and military conflict. Food security will not be achieved through rhetoric.
The United States has made clear that the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the Sustainable Development Goals impinge upon state sovereignty as a soft form of global governance, and we will no longer reaffirm them as a matter of course.
The United States also supports the elimination of discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, disability, national origin, and genetic information. The United States expects the United Nations to ensure that its documents and programming avoid using diversity, equity, and inclusion concepts and terminology at the expense of recognizing hard work, excellence, and individual achievement and upholding the equal dignity and respect of every person.
The UN can no longer afford to waste its resources where there is little or no impact. The number of reports, conferences, and negotiations has grown beyond the point of ineffectiveness and waste, to a level of dysfunction that makes it impossible to carry out vital work.
Accordingly, the United States requested a vote and voted “no” on this resolution.
Thank you.
