James Hamilton
USUNNY ECOSOC Advisor
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. Chair.
The United States thanks Facilitator Louis Mongkol of Thailand for his efforts to seek compromise and consensus. However, we cannot support the International Financial System for Development resolution this year.
The United States is troubled that many provisions in this resolution would raise the cost of capital worldwide by interfering in markets, undermining investor confidence, and debasing the integrity of credit ratings. Proposals to expand UN authority into areas best managed by sovereign states and specialized financial institutions, to direct the operations of the IMF and multilateral development banks, and to create new, duplicative mechanisms are not only inefficient but risk harming the very countries they purport to help.
For example, we are deeply concerned by provisions in this resolution that call for expanded UN involvement in sovereign debt restructuring. The United States believes that debt resolution is best managed through established mechanisms such as the Paris Club and the G20 Common Framework, which are grounded in transparency, cooperation, and fair burden sharing among creditors. UN-led processes risk politicizing debt negotiations, undermining market confidence, and delaying much-needed relief for countries in distress. Such approaches would ultimately raise the cost of capital and reduce access to financing for the very countries the resolution aims to support.
The United States supports an international financial system rooted in national sovereignty, fiscal responsibility, and market-based solutions. We believe countries should focus on increasing domestic productivity and resources, ensuring access to abundant and affordable energy, and fostering private sector-led growth.
These are the foundations for real economic progress—not bureaucratic overreach or top-down mandates. The United States is not an economic miracle because of any actions the UN has taken, but rather the will of a free people to allow markets to grow and innovation to flourish.
The United States has also made clear that we do not reaffirm the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development or the Sustainable Development Goals as a matter of course. We do not support aligning international financial institutions, official development assistance, or private capital mobilization with the SDGs. We believe that the proliferation of references to these frameworks in this resolution is misguided and does not advance the real economic interests of Member States.
We are concerned that the lack of consensus on these important issues undermines the Second Committee’s credibility and its ability to advance practical economic outcomes. The Committee’s work is weakened when it recycles contentious or counterproductive proposals that do not reflect the realities of today’s global economy.
We hope that future resolutions will reflect these priorities and offer a more constructive approach. For these reasons, the United States votes no.
Thank you.
