AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. Chair.
We start by expressing our gratitude to the facilitator, our colleague from Maldives; to UN Secretariat staff; and to fellow delegates for their professionalism throughout the negotiations.
The United States opposes this Second Committee resolution.
Roughly 700 million people remain without electricity access globally. Roughly 2 billion people have unreliable access. Despite the talk of the UN’s Sustainable Development Goals and calls for action on climate change, the percentage of energy poor has remained stubbornly high.
Abundant access to affordable, reliable, and secure energy is a core condition of economic development. Each year, millions of lives are lost because they lack access to energy sources that some label as “unsustainable.” To save lives and unlock economic prosperity, we must embrace all affordable, reliable, and secure energy options—including nuclear, natural gas, and coal. By restricting energy sources instead of expanding them, this resolution undermines global energy access, resulting in continued unmet needs and preventable deaths. By prioritizing climate politics over practical solutions, it risks higher costs, reduced energy reliability, and stalled progress on energy access and development—deepening energy and economic poverty.
What’s more, this resolution continues to include problematic language that does nothing to advance the United Nations’ core mandate of maintaining international peace and security. We highlight the consensus texts adopted by G7 and APEC Energy Ministers as significant examples of international cooperation on pressing energy issues.
The United States remains committed to unlocking energy independence and stands ready to partner with any country willing to depart from this outdated understanding of self-limiting potential. Thank you.
