Kali Gasteiger
U.S. Adviser to the Third Committee
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. Chair, The United States is pleased to join consensus on this resolution. We thank the Italian delegation for their work in shepherding this resolution through. The United States remains steadfast in prioritizing efforts to disrupt transnational criminal organizations that harm Americans. We are proud to have successfully incorporated language into this resolution calling for greater action to combat online scam operations in Southeast Asia. Additionally, the United States continues to lead efforts to disrupt alien smuggling networks that endanger lives and destabilize communities and we urge Member States to implement the U.S.-sponsored resolution from the May 2025 Commission on Crime Prevention and Criminal Justice, which calls for holding smugglers accountable for their crimes. We express our concern with the strategic effort by some delegations to give disproportionate attention to crimes that affect the environment in the hopes of justifying the need for a new protocol to the UN Convention against Transnational Organized Crime. We do not support a new protocol.
Furthermore, the United States notes there is no internationally agreed definition of “access to justice” and will not support any programs or interpretations of this concept that do not align with U.S. policy. We also strongly denounce efforts by Russia, Cuba, Iran, and others to broaden the scope of cybercrime in ways that suppress freedom of expression and dissent. The United States will continue to protect fundamental freedoms. The United States will continue to oppose the Sustainable Development Goals. We also firmly support efforts to protect women from crime and violence, while using clear and accurate language that centers on the needs of women and girls. Finally, let us be clear – this resolution does not prove there is sufficient political will to address the urgent threats posed by transnational criminal organizations. Where are the prosecutions of top criminals and corrupt officials? Where are the concerted efforts to disrupt the criminal networks trafficking drugs that devastate our communities? The United States will continue to lead and address impunity when other governments stand idly by, including through visa restrictions, sanctions regimes, or other tools. The stakes are too high for complacency. We need partners who are willing to act – not just talk. Thank you.
