Explanation of Vote for UNGA 80 Third Committee Resolution: “Situation of Human Rights in the Temporarily Occupied Territories of Ukraine, Including the Autonomous Republic of Crimea and the City of Sevastopol”
Louis Bono
U.S. Senior Adviser for European Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. Chair,
President Trump has made it clear from day one that there can be no military solution to this war – a war which has lasted for more than ten years, achieving nothing but senseless death and destruction.
We have and will continue to work with both sides to achieve an immediate ceasefire and a just, lasting peace. The United States remains committed to working in this body and others to ensure that there is no impunity for perpetrators of human rights abuses in this conflict.
We welcome the adoption of this resolution and thank its supporters as we continue our pressing work to eliminate the threat of war and the abuse of human rights in line with the principles and purposes of the Charter of the United Nations.
Notwithstanding our support for this resolution, the United States disassociates itself from any reference to the term “temporarily occupied territory” contained in the resolution. We also disassociate from any reference to the term “war of aggression” contained in the resolution.
As stated previously, the United States will continue to work to achieve an immediate ceasefire and a just, lasting peace. In line with this peace-driven approach, the United States judges the previously identified language is not
conducive to creating an environment for agreement on immediate or successful negotiations towards a durable peace agreement between Russia and Ukraine.
We are pleased to see this resolution adopted by the committee, and remain ready to work with all interested parties to advance the shared causes of peace and human rights.
