Michael Heath
U.S. Senior Adviser for East Asian and Pacific Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. Chair,
The United States is pleased to co-sponsor this resolution and we thank the co-drafters.
For years, the military regime in Myanmar has carried out atrocities against its own citizens, including those involving the detention and physical abuse of political opponents, the killing of unarmed civilians, sexual violence, and other appalling human rights violations and abuses.
We remain deeply concerned by continuous evidence, documented by the Independent Investigative Mechanism for Myanmar (IIMM), which, if proven accurate, indicates that both the military and certain armed groups engaged in the ongoing civil war in Myanmar have committed human rights violations and abuses. The IIMM’s continued role in identifying these concerning reports and promoting accountability for perpetrators is vital, and the United States supports its continued efforts to document relevant crimes involving human rights violations and abuses.
The United States is glad to see this resolution pass and urges all Member States to support further measures to prohibit the sale or transfer of weapons and other dual-use items that could be used to attack civilians trapped in this conflict through no fault of their own.
We again thank the penholders of this resolution for continuing to promote the salience of this issue to this body’s work.
I thank you.
