Maria Apud
U.S. Adviser to the Third Committee
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. Chair,
This resolution’s approach to artificial intelligence governance and digital technologies reflects a centralized, global governance model that fails to adequately respect the sovereign rights of states to regulate these technologies in accordance with their own national priorities. The United States supports responsible AI development but we cannot endorse language that promotes overly broad or undefined commitments to international regulatory frameworks without sufficient safeguards for transparency, accountability, and respect for national sovereignty. We also note that the UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights are non-binding and that their use in this context blurs the distinction between state obligations and corporate responsibilities under domestic law.
While the United States recognizes the importance of addressing disinformation, misinformation, and hate speech in the digital age, we believe the resolution’s references to the UN Principles on Information Integrity, , the Global Digital Compact, and the Pact for the Future risk advancing frameworks that could undermine state sovereignty and impose global governance models that are inconsistent with U.S. policy priorities. Free speech, even odious speech, is protected under the U.S. Constitution.
This fundamental freedom does not exist merely to protect widely accepted speech, but also to ensure protection of speech that bucks common norms.
The United States has made clear that the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) impinge upon state sovereignty as a soft form of global governance, and we will no longer reaffirm them as a matter of course.
The United States also supports the elimination of discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, disability, national origin, and genetic information. The United States expects the United Nations to ensure that its documents and programming avoid using diversity, equity, and inclusion concepts and terminology at the expense of recognizing hard work, excellence, and individual achievement and upholding the equal dignity and respect of every person.
The UN can no longer afford to waste its resources where there is little or no impact. The number of reports, conferences, and negotiations has grown beyond the point of ineffectiveness and waste, to a level of dysfunction that makes it impossible to carry out vital work.
Accordingly, the United States requests a vote and will vote “no” on this resolution.
Mr. Chair, colleagues, we hope to work together in advance of next year’s session of the Third Committee to make it more efficient, relevant, and effective. We refer you to the long form Explanation of Position that will be posted on our Mission’s website for more information. Thank you.
