Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
HARRIS FAULKNER: I will be moderating a panel at the U.S. Mission here, and the topic: the massacre of Christians in Nigeria, protecting them and religious freedom. Some experts call it outright genocide, and say the government is at best turning a blind eye, if not complicit. We’ll get into it. The numbers are horrifying. This year alone – now, I should say, from January to August. It’s mid-November. So, more people have died since this number obviously. Islamic terrorists have slaughtered more than 7,000 in the first eight months of this year. And since 2009 when sort of this is estimated to have begun, the annihilation of an entire population based on their faith, 125,000 have died. Also, since 2009, nearly 20,000 Christian churches have burned. And in addition to all the killing, those terrorists are also guilty of kidnapping and torturing thousands of people who are Christian. Some have survived to tell their stories. Knox Thames served as a State Department advisor for religious minorities under President G. W. Bush, Obama, and Trump.
KNOX THAMES: Yes, Muslims are being attacked as well, particularly by Boko Haram. There are brave – Nigerian Muslims are saying, this is not what our faith teaches. And they’re attacked for that. But time and time again, it’s been churches that have been attacked. It’s been places of worship, it’s been Christian communities, and so because of the death toll of Christians and others, we need to be doing more to encourage Nigeria to do these basic functions of governance, protect the vulnerable, hold perpetrators accountable.
FAULKNER: Well, the violence prompted President Trump recently to designate Nigeria as a CPC – that’s a Country of Particular Concern. He says the U.S. is ready and willing to take action.
PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We’re going to do things to Nigeria that Nigeria is not going to be happy about, and may very well go into that now disgraced country guns-a-blazing to completely wipe out the Islamic terrorists who are committing these horrible, horrible atrocities.
FAULKNER: In focus with me now, Mike Waltz, U.S. Ambassador to the United Nations. So, let’s start with what’s happening right on the ground, and what you at the U.S. Mission have picked up on, what President Trump has picked up on, is the urgency in this moment.
AMBASSADOR MIKE WALTZ: Well, I’m so grateful for President Trump to put his spotlight once again on this issue. But this isn’t new for him. In 2019, he was the first president ever, Harris, to convene all world leaders here at the United Nations during the General Assembly and say enough is enough, and it was a global call to protect religious freedom. But the situation has only worsened, particularly in Nigeria, and as you said, clergy are being beheaded. Churches are being burned, families chased out of their homes, simply for wearing a cross, simply for saying Christ is king and that they worship Jesus. And enough is enough. And I just can’t be more thankful for him. Not only has he done that, he has a Religious Liberty Task Force, he has a number of other things that he has done to eradicate Christian bias, not just around the world, even in our own federal government.
FAULKNER: I know that in his first term, he made Nigeria a Country of Particular Concern –
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: And Biden lifted it.
FAULKNER: – and he lifted it, and in that time, it’s gotten precipitously worse for those people on the ground. Look, a burning question today has to do with what really makes a difference, and you have invited rap superstar Nicki Minaj to come to the U.S. Mission at the United Nations. How did that come about?
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: Well, first again, it was her responding to President Trump, and, but, you know, what an unlikely duo, but what an amazing duo. She’s one of the most followed people in the world, and, of course, he’s the most famous world leader in the world. And it was actually our good friend Alex Bruesewitz, who said, why don’t you invite her over? And to our astonishment, she accepted. I have to tell you, my daughter now thinks what we’re doing here is cool for the first time, but importantly, she is reaching a whole other swath of people who may not follow these issues, and it’s going to be a great day.
