Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative to the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you to Sierra Leone for convening the Council today for this open debate. And it is especially an honor to have President Maada Bio presiding today over the Council. Welcome, sir.
Colleagues, the United States remains deeply alarmed by the rise in conflict related food insecurity and frankly the very grim picture of severe food insecurity in many nations, and many regions around the world.
As we look around the world, we see over and over again, oppressive regimes and their militaries, as well as terrorist groups and other non-state actors, destroying farms, destroying equipment, as well as blocking major trade routes and the humanitarian pipelines that are lifelines for hungry people. The result is increased food insecurity and increasingly the amounts of people starving. This must be stopped, colleagues. We must hold those responsible accountable for their actions.
And just looking around the world, for example, in the DRC, armed violence, particularly in the east, continues to displace families, continues to destroy fields, continues disrupt trade. Millions remain food insecure as armed groups attack civilians, block humanitarian routes, and undermine the ability of communities to buy or cultivate their own food.
In Sudan, the world’s largest humanitarian crisis, starvation has become a weapon, starvation has become rampant as the parties use food as a tactic of war. All parties – and I emphasize all parties – to the conflict must facilitate and allow rapid, safe, and unhindered humanitarian access throughout Sudan.
In Haiti, active humanitarian and lifesaving assistance continues to support families and children impacted and terrorized by gang violence. The same type of humanitarian assistance are helping families that have been devastated recently by Hurricane Melissa, and all of those suffering from all forms of food insecurity. Programming focuses on Haiti’s complex situation, providing life-saving assistance and rapid relief to communities, again, affected so horribly by gang violence and other forms of natural disasters. The United States has improved food security by supporting over 90,000 people with food, water, sanitation, shelter, and health services, hopefully paving the way for a better future for Haiti and for Haitians.
In Gaza, President Trump’s historic 20-point peace plan has already resulted in increased humanitarian aid to the people there. The United States will continue to work with the international community, with the UN, with so many nations represented here today to deliver aid at scale to help those who need it the most.
But just as an important statistic, in fiscal year 2025, the U.S. government provided over $400 million dollars in life-saving humanitarian assistance inside the West Bank and Gaza, of which over $300 million was for life-saving food and nutrition assistance. President Trump’s peace agreement has resulted in a dramatic increase in the flow of humanitarian aid into Gaza. An average now of over 660 trucks are entering Gaza daily since the ceasefire, and we will continue work to deliver this aid at scale.
The war in Ukraine has continued to exacerbate food insecurity globally. And it underscores the far reaching and long-lasting consequences of war on people’s basic needs and their basic ability to feed their family.
Unfortunately, colleagues, these are just a handful of countries and regions that are facing this scourge. The United States has proven again and again to be the most generous nation in the world. The American people contribute over half a trillion dollars in life-saving aid to charitable causes around the world, many of which help feed the hungry.
The United States has long shouldered the financial burden of addressing this scourge, and we urge other donors to increase critically needed assistance. But ultimately, the Deputy Secretary-General who joins us today is absolutely right. As she just said, only political solutions can lead to peace. Only political solutions can drive this scourge into the dustbin of history. And the United States will remain absolutely committed to driving these political solutions home, so that we never again have to see these hungry families that we’ve all described and that we pity here today.
Thank you, Mr. President.
