Ambassador Jennifer Locetta
Alternative Representative for Special Political Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. And many thanks to Sierra Leone for convening this important debate and thank you to all of our briefers for their contributions to this debate.
The United States welcomes this annual opportunity to hear the views of UN members about what aspects of the Council’s working methods serve us well, and where members believe there are areas for improvement.
Just last December the Council updated Note 507. We would like to thank Japan for its leadership in facilitating this process, which resulted in a number of updates to this important guide and resource.
The United States supports the continued use and implementation of the practices outlined in Note 507, which provide sufficiently clear guidelines for the work of the Council.
It is vitally important that the Council’s work, both substantive and procedural, serves the interests of all UN Member States.
In carrying out its primary responsibility for the maintenance of international peace and security, as provided in Article 24 of the UN Charter, the Security Council is acting on behalf of all Members of the United Nations.
As we look ahead to the selection of the next Secretary-General, the United States looks forward to a Secretary-General who shares this vision of returning the UN to its founding purpose of maintaining international peace and security.
We believe the process for the selection of such an important position should be purely merit-based with as wide a pool of candidates as possible. We therefore welcome candidates from all regional groupings.
Finally, we would like to thank the Security Council Affairs Division (SCAD) and other members of the Secretariat for their continued operational legwork behind the scenes, in support of the Council’s work.
Thank you.
