Ambassador Jeff Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Co-chairs, colleagues,
Efficient and effective delivery and measurable impact should be at the heart of everything we do in our work at the United Nations. To that end, the Secretary-General has rightly observed that meetings and reports are not ends in themselves—they are tools to achieve impact. Yet, far too often, the UN bureaucracy ignores (and even flouts) this common sense principle.
The United Nations is convening more meetings than ever before, commissioning more reports than anyone could possibly read, and allocating vast sums to sustain this machinery. In 2024 alone, the UN spent nearly $360 million on the direct costs of meetings and reports. Let me repeat – $360 million – not on maintaining international peace and security or providing lifesaving aid – but on meetings and reports.
These reports are now 40 percent longer than they were twenty years ago. Have they become 40 percent more useful, more insightful, or more actionable? Over 60 percent of UN reports cover recurring subjects—often with minimal change from year to year. Are these reports helping Member States make better, faster, or smarter decisions? I think we know the answer.
Colleagues, the United Nations can—indeed it must—do better. To start, this means consolidating and, where possible, eliminating overlapping meetings and duplicative reporting. It includes ending the nonsensical hosting of conferences in remote locations rather than UN campuses where the infrastructure, staff, and delegations already exist.
It also means shorter, sharper, and more strategic products, with clear executive summaries and data visualizations that tell us what we actually need to know. We welcome experimentation with new formats—concise updates, interactive dashboards, and live briefings—rather than lengthy documents that few have time to digest. More thorough estimates of program budget implications would enable us all to better determine whether new initiatives are worth the cost or whether they are redundant.
In our age of data analysis and instant communication, the duplication of work within the United Nations system is simply unacceptable, for it drains precious resources, inflates bureaucracy, and diverts time and talent from achieving real results. Getting “back to basics” is about discipline—focusing the UN’s time, people, and money on core mandates and measurable outcomes.
This same principle should guide the consolidation of global services—from IT to procurement to human resources—so we can redirect funds from administration to delivery and impact.
Colleagues, the United States looks forward to working with all Member States to achieve these essential, durable and consequential reforms.
Thank you.
