AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. Chairman. the United States requested a recorded vote on this resolution and is voting ‘NO’ due to a number of concerns we have with the text.
The United States believes transparent, accountable, and adaptable peace operations can be effective tools to address peace and security challenges.
We recognize the primacy of the UN Security Council for issues of international peace and security. We will not accept language in General Assembly resolutions that is overly prescriptive with regard to the Security Council.
Relatedly, this resolution improperly anticipates decisions that may result from the peacebuilding architecture review currently underway.
Even more, we do not see how this Committee’s annual consideration of this resolution is good use of the UN’s limited resources or how the annual reports requested in this resolution have materially improved the performance of Special Political Missions (SPM). These reports have essentially become activity reports.
There are other, sufficient fora for the consideration of most SPMs. For example, the lion’s share of the budget for cluster III SPMs is for missions which operate under Security Council mandates. The Security Council maintains oversight and strategic direction for these operations. Additionally, the Fifth Committee annually considers all SPMs as part of regular budget negotiations.
We note the resolution contains several references to the staffing composition of SPMs. UN resolutions should clearly refer to merit as the main consideration for staffing choices.
The United States also expects that UN resolutions avoid the term gender and use terminology that clearly refers to women and men, girls and boys. Similarly, this resolution’s reference to climate is vague. UN resolutions should focus on specific environmental threats and sensible environmental protections. Thank you, Mr. Chair.
