Lauren Lovelace
ECOSOC Counselor
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. Chair.
The United States welcomes the opportunity to address the crucial issue of revitalizing the Second Committee. We commend your leadership and the Bureau’s efforts to strengthen the Committee’s efficiency and effectiveness.
The United States continues to believe that the United Nations can be an important institution for solving international challenges, but it has too often strayed from its original purpose. The UN system has become overburdened by duplicative, overlapping mandates and a regrettable focus on process rather than results.
The time for endless talk about reform is over. The Committee must be streamlined and more disciplined. It must lead by example and focus squarely on advancing practical economic outcomes.
We are encouraged by delegations that have already taken steps to streamline their resolutions this session, and we thank them for their leadership.
As we reflect on the current state of the Committee, several challenges are evident: an ever-growing number of resolutions, many of which are introduced year in, year out with little change; lack of clarity and predictability around program budget implications; and rushed decision-making that undermines both quality and accountability.
These challenges are compounded by budget limitations that constrain the Committee’s ability to deliver on its core economic and financial mandate.
Revitalization is not an abstract exercise. It is a fundamental step toward restoring the Committee’s credibility and purpose. It is critical that this process aligns with the ongoing UN80 mandate review, ensuring coherence and efficiency across the system while eliminating redundancies.
Mr. Chair, the United States requests a clear, written roadmap outlining concrete steps for reform. We further ask that you instruct the Second Committee Bureau to guide and coordinate the revitalization process, acting as a task force to identify overlaps, streamline work, and make practical recommendations to strengthen the Committee’s focus and effectiveness.
We further request that this Bureau-led task force hold regular consultations to gather Member States’ views and present them to the President of the General Assembly.
Mr. Chair, the Second Committee has an opportunity to demonstrate what meaningful reform can look like: fewer but stronger resolutions; leaner processes that respect the time and resources of all delegations; and decisions that reflect the Committee’s core mission of promoting global economic stability and cooperation.
The United States stands ready to work constructively to achieve a Second Committee that is focused, transparent, and capable of delivering real results for the people we serve.
