Explanation of Vote for UNGA 80 Third Committee Resolution: “Report of the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees, Questions Relating to Refugees, Returnees and Displaced Persons and Humanitarian Questions”
Maria Apud
U.S. Adviser to the Third Committee
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. Chair,
The United States has serious reservations about the proposed resolution.
We regret that there was no meaningful, substantive negotiation in Geneva on the core elements of this resolution, and we lament that there was no suggestion to have such a negotiation in New York.
The United States is disappointed that the facilitator of the resolution shared the text with New York missions and represented it as having “been negotiated in Geneva,” which is plainly untrue.
We stand firmly against processes that selectively silence individual Member States for the sake of convenience.
Moreover, we believe this resolution fails to adequately address the paramount principles of migration management, which we have consistently maintained must be border security, national sovereignty, and the right of every country to set their own migration policy.
We urge other delegations to this body to question why you would support this effort, as well.
Accordingly, the United States requests a vote and will vote “no” on this resolution.
Thank you.
