John Kelley
Political Counselor
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Mr. Chairman, the United States requested a recorded vote on resolutions A and B and is voting ‘NO’ due to a number of concerns we have with the texts.
First, the United States will not accept suppression of the freedom of expression under the guise of combatting “disinformation” and “misinformation.” Government censorship of speech is intolerable in a free society, and these terms are tools to police and moderate public debate.
The United States is making every effort to protect freedom of expression, and we will not tolerate efforts to trample it at home or abroad. We will not shy away from calling out countries imposing censorship tactics, such as government-imposed shutdowns of the Internet and undue restrictions on freedom of expression online. We call on all other member states to strengthen the free flow of information.
Second, we remain vigilant against attempts to inject political or ideological language into United Nations resolutions. This resolution contains numerous references that serve only to shield Beijing from criticisms over its governance system and human rights abuses.
Similarly, the resolutions contain many references to “developing countries.” We are concerned that countries, like China, are misappropriating the term to gain advantages in the UN system. We urge the use of more clearly defined terminology that reflects actual national economic conditions. Technical assistance should be based on objective criteria, not vague and inconsistent labels.
Third, the repeated references to the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the Sustainable Development Goals impinge upon state sovereignty as a soft form of global governance. We no longer reaffirm them as a matter of course.
Fourth, in lieu of general references to climate and climate change, UN resolutions should focus on specific environmental threats and sensible environmental protections. Similarly, the references to gender are ambiguous and unclear. The United States expects that UN resolutions use terminology that clearly refers to women and men, girls and boys.
Finally, the United States questions whether yearly consideration of these resolutions is a good use of the UN’s limited resources. Next year, we look forward to engaging with the Committee on Information to reduce the frequency to once every two years and to formulate a more focused, unambiguous, and apolitical report.
Thank you.
