Ambassador Jeff Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Madame Chair,
In its 80th year, the United Nations needs to get back to basics, to renew its founding mission to maintain international peace and security, and to rebuild trust across the world that it can be a responsible steward of the precious resources that Member States entrust to it. To that end, we must urgently confront the fact that the UN is currently spending an unsustainable $16 billion a year on staff compensation across the common system.
In 2021, the General Assembly mandated a comprehensive review of the common system compensation package, due in 2026. The United States is concerned that the ongoing comprehensive review lacks the necessary guidance and sense-of-urgency to meet the moment. To put it another way, we can’t wait until next year. We must act now.
During this Main Session, we have the opportunity to instruct the International Civil Service Commission to present options that achieve material and durable reforms across the common system. First and foremost, staff compensation costs must be brought into line with common sense benchmarks. UN staff currently out-earn their civil service counterparts in every Member State, including the United States, by wide margins on base salary alone.
And unlike their civil service counterparts, many UN staff also receive costly dependency allowances, education grants for children, and duplicative field allowances. Together with rising health insurance costs, these are the cost drivers that the General Assembly must address now.
We recognize that in the past, the General Assembly has relied on the Secretariat, the ICSC, and the CEB to take the lead on evaluating cost drivers and long-term trends in staff compensation costs. Unfortunately, we are now confronted with the unsustainable costs that this business-as-usual approach has yielded, and that is why we must act during this Main Session.
Colleagues, we recognize that disrupting business-as-usual is jarring for some, and that achieving consensus on compensation reform is a heavy lift. That said, we have all the data we need to understand the scope of the problem and mandate common sense solutions. The United States looks forward to working with all Member States during this Main Session to achieve these necessary, durable and consequential reforms.
Thank you.
