Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative to the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. And thank you, Assistant Secretary-General Pobee and Special Envoy Cong, for your briefings today and for your valuable and ongoing contributions to advancing peace and security in Abyei.
The United States is deeply concerned about the Rapid Support Force’s (RSF) impediments to UNISFA and the Joint Border Verification Monitoring Mechanism. The RSF’s efforts to gain legitimacy through the illegitimate arrest and detainment of Abyei residents and officials further violates the demilitarized status and must cease immediately.
Security concerns and operational constraints by the Rapid Support Force, Sudan Armed Forces, and Sudanese and South Sudanese authorities have further forced the Joint Border Verification Monitoring Mechanism to temporarily relocate its Kadugli Headquarters to Abyei and close the Gok Machar Headquarters, obstructing its mandated tasks.
Meanwhile, the South Sudan People’s Defense Forces and South Sudan National Police Service continue to hinder UNISFA’s patrols, its logistics operations, its establishment of a temporary base, and to occupy UN and civilian facilities.
The United States calls on all parties involved to cooperate with the Joint Border Verification Monitoring Mechanism and to ensure conditions for the full reactivation of its Kadugli and Gok Machar Headquarters and to cease all obstructions to Joint Border Verification Monitoring Mechanism and UNISFA’s mandate.
The United States strongly urges the relevant authorities to withdraw all [unauthorized] armed forces and armed elements from the Abyei area, within their respective purviews, and urges Council Members, urges the African Union, and the Sudanese and South Sudanese governments to strengthen their efforts to support UNISFA and take all necessary steps to ensure the full demilitarization of Abyei.
While we recognize Sudan and South Sudan are facing historic, significant internal challenges and instability, it does not absolve authorities of their responsibility to implement their key commitments under the 2011 peace agreement to maintain Abyei as a demilitarized zone, to establish temporary administrative and security arrangements — including the Joint Abyei Police Service — and determine the final status of Abyei.
The people of Abyei have been waiting 14 years for the establishment of the temporary administrative and security arrangements, including the Joint Abyei Police Service, and a determination of Abyei’s final status.
On UNISFA, further long-standing delays to approve the deployment of the full 640 UN police personnel and three formed police units, as well as to support the appointment of a civilian Deputy Head of Mission, both of which have been authorized by this Council, continue to obstruct the full implementation of UNISFA’s mandate.
The parties’ continued inaction and deliberate undermining of the peace agreement and UNISFA’s mandate raises serious doubts for the United States about continuing its support for UNISFA’s mandate unless Sudan and South Sudan demonstrate immediate and tangible progress on the following conditions:
First, the United States calls on Sudan and South Sudan authorities to promptly take concrete steps to withdraw all [unauthorized] forces from the demilitarized zone. The United States calls on Sudan and South Sudan to establish the Joint Abyei Police Service, and implement the temporary administrative and security arrangements.
Second, we strongly urge Sudan and South Sudan to facilitate the full implementation of UNISFA’s mandate. This includes all parties immediately authorizing visas for the 640 UN police personnel and three formed police units, that are supporting the appointment of a civilian Deputy Head of Mission, and resuming the Joint Political and Security Mechanism meetings without delay.
Facilitating the prompt withdrawal of unauthorized forces and issuance of visas for the 640 UN police personnel and the three formed police units should be prioritized by the parties as an essential first step.
These actions will enable UNISFA’s military component to focus on [maintaining] Abyei as a demilitarized zone, which is critical amid rising encroachment of the RSF and the South Sudan People’s Defense Forces.
Sudan and South Sudan must now uphold their commitment to the 2011 peace agreement and UNISFA’s mandate to secure the United States’ support of this renewal.
I thank you, Mr. President.
