John Kelley
Political Counselor
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. Thank you to the Chair.
I want to express our appreciation to the Panel of Experts for its thorough reporting and helpful recommendations.
The Panel’s 2025 Final Report is a wake-up call for this Council and the world.
The evidence is clear: systematic violations of Security Council sanctions continue, allowing the Houthis to acquire arms and related materiel, dual-use components and advanced electronics to fuel weapons programs enabling the Houthis to menace their neighbors and restrict international commerce in the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden.
These transfers fuel Houthi attacks with drones and missiles that endanger commercial shipping, destabilize the region, and put innocent lives at risk.
These transfers enabled deadly Houthi terrorist attacks on civilians in Israel, including the September drone attacks on a commercial center and airport in Eilat.
Such violations, unaddressed, embolden the Houthis to threaten their neighbors, including recent threats to renew attacks against Saudi critical infrastructure and civilians.
In addition to arms transfers enabling the Houthi war machine, the Panel has exposed the Houthis’ sophisticated financial support networks underwriting the group’s ability to conduct attacks.
If we want to end this conflict, we must disrupt these illicit transfers of financial and material resources.
The list of Houthi attacks on international shipping in recent months helps illustrate the threat posed to international peace and security, and the need for action to address it.
In July, the MAGIC SEAS was attacked and sank off Al-Hudaydah; days later, the ETERNITY C was struck by unmanned vehicles and rockets, leaving crew dead, wounded, or missing—and some in Houthi custody.
In September, the Houthis also attacked the MINERVAGRACHT, injuring crew members.
As the UNSC engages in negotiations to renew the Yemen sanctions regime, we call on other members of the Council to support efforts to strengthen measures to address these threats and stem the flow of arms and funds to the Houthis.
These measures are necessary to protect the global economy, defend freedom of navigation, and create space for diplomacy.
The Panel’s call for expanded cargo inspections, including at sea, is both timely and necessary.
We support this recommendation and urge the Council to establish a maritime enforcement mechanism for Yemen in the spirit of Operation IRINI’s mission in Libya.
Improved enforcement of the arms embargo is clearly needed, particularly against continuing shipments of arms and related materiel coming from Iran in violation of UN sanctions pertaining to Yemen and Iran.
Additionally, the Panel’s report details the continuing flow of dual-use components into Yemen intended for military use in Houthi weapons programs. It notes that many of these shipments originate in China.
We urge the Council and its members to take decisive action against these dual-use transfers.
As the Panel noted, law enforcement coordination and information sharing is essential, especially with the UN Verification and Inspection Mechanism (UNVIM).
We applaud the Malta-based MUSE project for its formal arrangement of support to UNVIM and look forward to its upcoming December meeting for flag registry compliance directors, where the Panel’s expertise will provide an invaluable contribution.
The Panel’s report is not just a document; it is a call to action.
This Council must uphold its mandate and resolutions to ensure peace and security, including in the Red Sea and Gulf of Aden.
The people of Yemen deserve a future free from violence.
Thank you, Mr. President.
