Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
BRIAN KILMEADE: President Trump not ruling out sending U.S. troops in Nigeria as thousands of Christians are brutally massacred by Islamic terrorists.
LAWRENCE JONES: Trump support, earning rare praise from rapper Nicki Minaj, who thanked him in a public post for, quote, taking this seriously.
AINSLEY EARHARDT: U.S. Ambassador to the UN Michael Waltz followed up with an invite to the embassy to discuss the issue, to which Minaj said she would be honored. Ambassador Michael Waltz joins us now. Ambassador, thank you so much for being here. For people who aren’t aware, we’re all hearing about the killings of Christians in Nigeria. How bad of a problem is it? Tell us what’s happening.
AMBASSADOR MIKE WALTZ: Oh, it’s a horrendous problem. And you have to give President Trump a lot of credit, he’s been on this religious freedom issue for quite some time. In fact, he was the first head of state, the first President ever, to host an international religious freedom conference at the United Nations back in 2019. He designated then Nigeria as a country of particular concern. Guess what? The Biden administration lifted it. And what you have is a terrorist organization, particularly in the north in Nigeria, Boko Haram, that is literally burning churches, massacring people, torturing people, running them out of their homes to the tunes of tens, if not hundreds of thousands. And so, President Trump put a spotlight on it. And you know, really kudos and thanks to someone like Nicki Minaj, as a Christian, but as someone with the following that she has, and with a lot of followers who may not be paying attention to these kinds of issues, to kind of double down. That’s a collaboration I don’t know that I would have foreseen seen over the weekend. Yeah.
KILMEADE: But how about – here’s some of these stats. They kind of blow you away. The Christians persecuted in Nigeria. How about 18,000 churches burned since 2009. 50,000 killed since that same year. Five million displaced. 7000 killed in 2025. This is raging right now. Can you gauge – what are the possible retribution? What could you actually do to stop this?
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: Well, I served in Nigeria when in my special forces unit in 2015. We provided them training. We were training their navy seals on how to get after Boko Haram. We provided them arms and other types of security assistance.
EARHARDT: You helped rescue those ladies, the girls.
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: Remember the girls, the 300 girls. And Ainsley, it is just heartbreaking and disgusting. The vast majority were sold as sex slaves and brides into these Islamist organizations like Boko Haram and ISIS. So, we work a lot with the Nigerians. What the President is saying is, I’m not going to put this as a back burner issue. This is an upfront issue. And Nigeria, you need to take it more seriously. And look, they’ve been fighting these insurgents, but it’s been kind of up and down over the years. And again, when you have the Biden administration come in and say, this isn’t important, we’re going to, we’re going to lift that designation. Remember when they did that with the Houthis? Trump says they’re terrorists, and Biden says, no, they’re not. And, you know, and then they’re out of control.
JONES: Why isn’t there any moral clarity from the United Nations? You know, we don’t see that when the Jews are being attacked. We don’t see when the Christians are being attacked. This is what they’re supposed to be doing –
KILMEADE: Only when the Palestinians are attacked.
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: Yeah. Right? By the way, Brian, where are all the protests on the streets and in our universities and elsewhere when you have hundreds of thousands of Christians – just, I’ll get to that point. But just on the Christian persecution, 78 countries, 330 million Christians being persecuted around the world. It is the most persecuted religion across the Middle East and Africa and elsewhere. And to your point, relative silence from the international community. It is in the UN Charter as a basic human right. It’s one of the things that Nicki Minaj is going to come over, and we’re going to have her talk to the UN, talk to our experts, and actually hear from some of these pastors and others that have been so horribly persecuted. And I just, I really welcome her engagement, her spotlight.
EARHARDT: What caused her engagement?
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: Well, I think one it was President Trump. Only he can put a spotlight like he can on these issues. You know, we’re going kind of back and forth now. I’m not sure what actually got her to focus on this.
JONES: You’re about to find out.
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: I’m about to find out, and stay tuned. You know, and as a Christian, I’m just thrilled that she’s staying engaged.
EARHARDT: I am too. I love that she’s doing this. I hope other celebrities do too. Thank you so much, Ambassador.
